# Review: ChessPlugin

The spec describes a modelled design: a `Board` of typed squares with a `status`, a `Game` with a
lifecycle (`waiting`/`active`/`finished`) and a winner, a Lobby, `ChessEngine` and `GameStore`
services, and pure `getLegalMoves`/`validateMove`/`applyMove` ops composed by `submitMove`. The code
is built differently. The chess state is a `Chess.State` holding only `pgn` and `fen`, referenced
as the variant of plugin-game's generic `Game` (`name`, `description`, `players`, `variant`).
chess.js is called directly for rules, check, mate and stalemate. The board UI is
`@dxos/react-ui-gameboard`, wrapped by plugin-chess's `Chessboard`. Drops go to
`ChessModel.makeMove` and write the PGN directly. They do not go through an operation.

What exists works: games start from the standard position, illegal moves are rejected with no
write, promotion has a selector, check/mate/stalemate/draw are shown in the move history, and the
`Move` (`org.dxos.operation.chess.move`) and `Play` operations are registered. Nothing of the Lobby,
join flow, resignation, game status or winner exists. Test coverage is thin: `plugin.test.ts`
checks module activation and `Print`, and `react-ui-gameboard`'s `chess.test.ts` checks only that a
piece map builds. No test covers `Move`, `Play` or any acceptance scenario.

Of 49 items: 0 done, 8 untested, 2 partial, 18 diverged, 21 missing.

